---
// <BcnEvidenceTargets> — the RIGHT column of the evidence drawer, and the DROP TARGET.
// Each action is a card that holds the evidence proving it.
//
// THE JOIN IS A DRAG. Evidence is dragged from the left column onto an action card, which
// appends it to that card's sub-list. This replaces the earlier model where suggestions
// added whole action ROWS and the join was implied by both lists being populated — the
// association is now an explicit, visible act with a visible result.
//
// Ported from the Setup Wizard's Actions step (project-setup/actions-step), the app's
// existing drag-to-associate surface: a tinted receiving card, the source
// row dimmed while it travels, an attached sub-list with per-row remove, and a hint line
// that doubles as the empty state.
//
// NO DOUBLE-ATTACH. An action already holding the dragged evidence is dimmed and inert for
// the duration of that drag, so the rule is visible before you try rather than enforced
// after you drop.
//
// SUGGESTIONS ATTACH, THEY DO NOT PROPOSE. Find matches (in the evidence column's footer)
// may pull new action cards into this list, and attaches the relevant evidence to them.
// The "Suggested" marker sits on the individual ATTACHMENT, not on the card: the action was
// not suggested, this particular pairing was.
//
// SCOPE IS ABSOLUTE. The list only ever contains actions in the active component, and now
// also narrows by phase. Both filters live in this column's header because they govern
// this column and nothing else, and both are guarded while associations are unsaved.
//
// VOCABULARY. "Actions", never "implementations" (prod hides that distinction). "Find
// matches", "Suggested" — never "AI", no persona, no sparkle glyphs. The utility is
// plumbing, and it is styled like plumbing.
//
// WHY THE CODE CHIP IS NOT COLOUR-CODED BY ACTION TYPE. It was tried. Beacon colour-codes
// by ENTITY, not by flavour: the theme ships one four-colour entity ramp (--color-source →
// --color-commitment → --color-requirement → --color-action, ported verbatim from prod's
// Setup Wizard), and BcnCommitmentBadge exists specifically so every surface showing a
// commitment code renders the SAME chip in the commitment colour. Tinting these by
// monitoring / tracking / reporting would put a second, conflicting meaning on the one
// element that already has a settled one, and would break the cross-surface match with the
// modules, the timeline popovers and the tracker rows. Frequency and period stay plain
// meta text for the same reason — they are descriptive, not stateful, and Beacon reserves
// coloured chips for entity identity (BcnCommitmentBadge) and status (BcnStatusChip).
//
// Every row for every component is pre-rendered and hidden; the client script reveals the
// ones in scope and in the list. Astro is compile-time, so this is the same
// pre-render-and-toggle strategy BcnGuidanceDrawer uses.
//
// bcn-lego-checked: esa-select drives both filters; esa-card is every action card's
// chrome; esa-icon-button removes a card and removes an attachment; esa-badge is the quiet
// "Suggested" marker on an attachment; esa-empty-state is the nothing-selected state;
// esa-loading-spinner is the working state; bcn-commitment-badge is the code chip and
// bcn-evidence-search the field. Checked Ecology for a drag-to-associate lego — none
// exists — and Beacon prod, whose own implementation is Angular CDK (cdkDropList), not
// portable here; the HTML5 drag API is the equivalent and the VISUAL contract is copied
// from it exactly. bcn-evidence-targets is the reusable home; its own CSS is the panel
// frame, the card, the drop states and the attached sub-list.
import EsaCard from '@esa/ecology/esa-card.astro';
import EsaIcon from '@esa/ecology/esa-icon.astro';
import EsaBadge from '@esa/ecology/esa-badge.astro';
import EsaIconButton from '@esa/ecology/esa-icon-button.astro';
import EsaEmptyState from '@esa/ecology/esa-empty-state.astro';
import EsaLoadingSpinner from '@esa/ecology/esa-loading-spinner.astro';
import BcnCommitmentBadge from './BcnCommitmentBadge.astro';
import BcnDisclosureToggle from './BcnDisclosureToggle.astro';
import BcnCountChip from './BcnCountChip.astro';

import BcnEvidenceSearch from './BcnEvidenceSearch.astro';
import { ACTIONS, TYPE_LABEL, phaseName } from '../../data/evidence-drawer';
import { scopeIcon } from '../../data/global-search';
---

<section class="bcn-ev-targets" aria-labelledby="bcn-ev-targets-title">
  <header class="bcn-ev-targets__head">
    <h3 class="bcn-ev-targets__title" id="bcn-ev-targets-title">
      <EsaIcon name="radar" paths={scopeIcon('actions')} size="sm" />
      Actions
      <span class="bcn-ev-targets__count" data-targets-count aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </h3>
  </header>

  <!-- The three facets on a ruled row, NOT in a card: its bottom border is the same
       hairline the Evidence column's tab strip draws, at the same height, so the two
       columns share one line across the seam instead of each starting differently.
       The scope lives HERE, not in the drawer header: it governs this column and nothing
       else. Component and Phase are the dimensions the Setup Wizard's Actions step filters
       on; all three are guarded while associations are unsaved (see evidence-drawer.ts). -->
  <div class="bcn-ev-targets__filters">
    <span class="bcn-ev-targets__filter">
      <span class="bcn-ev-targets__flabel" id="bcn-ev-flabel-component">Component</span>
      <esa-select
        data-evidence-component
        size="sm"
        searchable
        aria-labelledby="bcn-ev-flabel-component"></esa-select>
    </span>
    <span class="bcn-ev-targets__filter">
      <span class="bcn-ev-targets__flabel" id="bcn-ev-flabel-phase">Phase</span>
      <esa-select data-targets-phase size="sm" aria-labelledby="bcn-ev-flabel-phase"></esa-select>
    </span>
    <span class="bcn-ev-targets__filter">
      <span class="bcn-ev-targets__flabel" id="bcn-ev-flabel-type">Type</span>
      <esa-select data-targets-type size="sm" aria-labelledby="bcn-ev-flabel-type"></esa-select>
    </span>
  </div>

  <div class="bcn-ev-targets__search">
    <BcnEvidenceSearch
      hook="targets"
      label="Search actions in this component"
      placeholder="Search actions in this component"
    />
  </div>

  <div class="bcn-ev-targets__scroll">
    <!-- Working state for Find matches — replaced by rows the moment it resolves. -->
    <div class="bcn-ev-targets__working" data-targets-working hidden>
      <EsaLoadingSpinner size="sm" />
      <span class="type-body-small">Reading the evidence and checking actions in this component…</span>
    </div>

    <!-- Find matches must never finish silently. A utility that runs and then does nothing
         visible reads as broken, and the two ways it legitimately finds nothing — no
         evidence staged, and nothing new in scope — are different answers that deserve
         different sentences. -->
    <p class="bcn-ev-targets__notice type-body-small" data-targets-notice hidden></p>

    <!-- The one list: searched rows and suggested rows together. -->
    <ul class="bcn-ev-targets__list" data-targets-list>
      {ACTIONS.map((a) => (
        <li
          class="bcn-ev-targets__item"
          data-target-row={a.id}
          data-component={a.componentId}
          data-tier="manual"
          hidden
        >
          <EsaCard variant="outlined">
            <div class="bcn-ev-row">
              <div class="bcn-ev-row__head">
                <div class="bcn-ev-row__main">
                <div class="bcn-ev-row__titlerow">
                  <BcnDisclosureToggle hook="data-card-toggle" value={a.id} label={`Collapse ${a.name}`} />
                  <!-- Unsaved marker for the CARD, shown when anything about this
                       action's evidence is outstanding — an addition OR a removal. A removal
                       leaves no row behind to carry the pink treatment, so without this the
                       card would look settled while still holding a pending change. -->
                  <span class="bcn-ev-row__dot" data-action-unsaved hidden aria-hidden="true"></span>
                  <span class="bcn-ev-row__name">{a.name}</span>
                  <span class="bcn-ev-row__codes">
                    <BcnCommitmentBadge code={a.code} size="sm" />
                    {(a.otherCodes?.length ?? 0) > 0 && (
                      <esa-popover class="bcn-ev-row__morepop" position="bottom" trigger="hover" offset={6}>
                        <span class="bcn-ev-row__more">
                          <BcnCommitmentBadge
                            code={`+ ${a.otherCodes!.length} more`}
                            size="sm"
                            tone="neutral"
                          />
                        </span>
                        <div slot="content" class="bcn-ev-row__poplist">
                          <p class="bcn-ev-row__poptitle type-caption">Commitments</p>
                          <ul>
                            {[a.code, ...(a.otherCodes ?? [])].map((c) => (
                              <li><BcnCommitmentBadge code={c} size="sm" /></li>
                            ))}
                          </ul>
                        </div>
                      </esa-popover>
                    )}
                    <!-- Right of the codes, INSIDE the same group: the count belongs to this
                         action's identity line, and grouping it with the codes means it
                         wraps with them rather than stranding itself when the row is tight. -->
                    <BcnCountChip
                      name="paperclip"
                      paths={scopeIcon('evidence')}
                      count={0}
                      label="No evidence attached yet"
                      hook="data-action-evcount"
                    />
                  </span>
                  <span class="bcn-ev-row__spacer" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                  <span class="bcn-ev-row__tags">
                    <EsaBadge value={TYPE_LABEL[a.type]} size="sm" />
                    <EsaBadge value={phaseName(a.phase)} size="sm" />
                  </span>
                </div>
                </div>
                <div class="bcn-ev-row__side">
                  <span class="bcn-ev-row__dismiss" data-target-dismiss={a.id}>
                    <EsaIconButton icon="x" label={`Remove ${a.name}`} size="sm" />
                  </span>
                </div>
              </div>

              <!-- The collapsible body sits BELOW the head rather than inside it, so the
                   list runs the card's full width — under the dismiss button — and is
                   indented on the left to start where the title starts, not where the
                   chevron does. -->
              <div class="bcn-ev-row__body" data-card-body>
                <ul class="bcn-ev-attached" data-attached-list={a.id}></ul>
                <p class="bcn-ev-row__hint type-body-small" data-attached-hint>
                  Drag evidence here to attach it.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </EsaCard>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <!-- An attached-evidence row, pre-rendered from the LEGOS once and cloned per
         attachment by the controller. Astro's legos are compile-time, so a template is how
         a runtime-built list still gets real esa-badge / esa-icon-button markup instead of
         hand-written copies of it (the same trick BcnGuidanceDrawer uses for its avatar). -->
    <template data-attached-row>
      <li class="bcn-ev-attached__row">
        <span class="bcn-ev-attached__name"></span>
        <span class="bcn-ev-attached__mark" hidden>
          <EsaBadge value="Suggested" size="sm" />
        </span>
        <span class="bcn-ev-attached__remove">
          <EsaIconButton icon="x" label="Remove this evidence from the action" size="sm" />
        </span>
      </li>
    </template>

    <!-- Nothing on the list yet. -->
    <div class="bcn-ev-targets__empty" data-targets-empty>
      <EsaEmptyState
        title="No actions selected"
        description="Search above to add one, or run Find matches once you have added evidence."
        size="sm"
      />
    </div>

  </div>

</section>

<style>
  /* The column sits one value BELOW the evidence side — the same sunken gray the file
     dropzone uses. It reads as the surface things are filed INTO, and it makes the white
     action cards on it read as objects rather than as regions of the panel. */
  .bcn-ev-targets {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    min-height: 0;
    height: 100%;
    background: var(--color-surface-sunken);
  }
  /* Title only now — the filters moved into the bar below, so this matches the Evidence
     column's header box exactly. Keep min-height in sync with .bcn-ev-staging__head. */
  .bcn-ev-targets__head {
    flex: none;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--spacing-300);
    min-height: 60px;
    padding: var(--spacing-350, 0.875rem) var(--spacing-400);
  }
  /* The ruled filter row. --color-border is the same token esa-tab-layout draws its
     underline with, and the padding matches the tab strip's, so the two hairlines are one
     continuous line across the seam. */
  .bcn-ev-targets__filters {
    /* The row tucks UP into the header's slack by --_lift and grows by the same amount, so
       the controls sit nearer the title while the rule stays exactly on the tab line. The
       lift is spent above; what it buys is breathing room below the controls. The header
       itself does not move, so the two column titles stay level across the seam. */
    --_lift: var(--spacing-050, 0.125rem);
    flex: none;
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    /* NOWRAP is structural, not cosmetic: this row's height is what puts its hairline on
       the Evidence column's tab line, and a wrapped row is a taller row. The boxes below
       are all allowed to shrink, so a narrow drawer compresses them instead of wrapping. */
    flex-wrap: nowrap;
    gap: var(--spacing-400);
    margin: calc(var(--_lift) * -1) var(--spacing-400) 0;
    /* Height from the TAB LEGO'S OWN token (its strip is one tab tall plus the 1px rule),
       not a measured pixel — if the tab size changes, this line follows it. */
    min-height: calc(var(--tab-layout-height-sm, 36px) + 1px + var(--_lift));
    box-sizing: border-box;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }
  /* The search sits BELOW the filter card, between it and the list it searches. */
  .bcn-ev-targets__search {
    flex: none;
    /* The shared gap (see #bcn-evidence-drawer) — the left column reaches the same distance
       by pulling up out of the tab panel's padding, so both fields land on one line. */
    padding: var(--bcn-ev-search-gap) var(--spacing-400) 0;
  }
  /* Label BESIDE the control, not above it — the same inline treatment permit-tracking's
     filter bar uses (.bcn-filterbar__group / __label). Stacking esa-select's own `label`
     would add a row to this header and break its alignment with the Evidence column's. */
  .bcn-ev-targets__filter {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--spacing-200);
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .bcn-ev-targets__flabel {
    flex: none;
    /* 13px is a DELIBERATE hard-code, not a missing token: the scale steps 14px
       (--type-size-150) straight to 12px (--type-size-100), and 12px is under the floor
       design-principles sets for the smallest legible meta text. 13px is that floor exactly
       — the smallest these can go and still be inside the system.
       Colour steps UP as the size steps down (tertiary → secondary), per the same rule:
       "never fainter: darken as you shrink". A 13px tertiary label is the combination the
       house style specifically warns against. */
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  /* Each box is sized by FLEX BASIS, not min-width: esa-select's intrinsic width is wider
     than its shortest option, so a min-width floor could not make Type narrower — only a
     basis can. min-width:0 everywhere is what lets them compress rather than wrap. */
  .bcn-ev-targets__filter esa-select {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 var(--_filter-box);
    min-width: 0;
  }
  /* Component names are the long ones here — "Southern Forebay & Pumping Plant" is twice
     the width of any phase or type — so this box gets a bigger floor AND the row's leftover
     width, while Phase and Type stay at their content size. The other two are closed
     vocabularies with short labels; only this one grows with the project. */
  /* All three boxes are the SAME width — one fixed track, so they are equal by construction
     rather than by three sizes that happen to agree. A flex BASIS was tried first and did
     not hold: the boxes sit in three separate inline-flex containers, so they shrank
     unevenly against their own labels instead of settling on the basis. A fixed width is
     the only thing that makes "same width" actually true here. */
  .bcn-ev-targets__filters {
    --_filter-box: 200px;
  }
  /* Count of actions in view — the same signal the wizard's column header carries. */
  .bcn-ev-targets__count {
    font-size: var(--type-size-150, 0.875rem);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-regular);
    color: var(--color-text-tertiary);
  }
  /* Matches BcnEvidenceStaging's header exactly — the two panels are peers, so their
     headings must resolve to the same rendered size, not just the same intent. */
  .bcn-ev-targets__title {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--spacing-200);
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--type-size-250);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    line-height: 1.25;
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
  }
  .bcn-ev-targets__title :global(.esa-icon) {
    flex: none;
    color: var(--color-text-tertiary);
  }


  /* The search belongs to the header, not the list: no gap above it, a clear one below.
     Keep the top padding in sync with .bcn-ev-staging__scroll. */
  .bcn-ev-targets__scroll {
    flex: 1;
    min-height: 0;
    overflow-y: auto;
    padding: var(--spacing-400) var(--spacing-400) var(--spacing-400);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-300);
  }

  .bcn-ev-targets__working {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--spacing-250, 0.625rem);
    padding: var(--spacing-300);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border-light);
    border-radius: var(--radius-200);
    background: var(--color-background);
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }
  .bcn-ev-targets__working[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  .bcn-ev-targets__notice {
    margin: 0;
    padding: var(--spacing-250, 0.625rem) var(--spacing-300);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border-light);
    border-radius: var(--radius-200);
    background: var(--color-background);
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }
  .bcn-ev-targets__notice[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  /* Same gap as the staged-evidence list on the other side. */
  .bcn-ev-targets__list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-300);
  }
  .bcn-ev-targets__list:not(:has(> li:not([hidden]))) {
    display: none;
  }
  .bcn-ev-targets__empty[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  /* ── A row, inside a card — same shell as an evidence card on the other side, so the two
     halves of the workspace read as one thing being joined to another. Cards in a gapped
     stack, not divider rows: an item you have chosen is an object, and the card edge is
     what makes it feel selectable and dismissible. ── */
  .bcn-ev-targets__item[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }
  /* The card is a COLUMN: a head row, then a full-width body beneath it. */
  .bcn-ev-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }
  /* CENTRE, not flex-start: the dismiss button is taller than the title row, so aligning
     to the top left the extra height below the title and a collapsed card sat lopsided —
     more space beneath the title than above it. */
  .bcn-ev-row__head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--spacing-300);
  }
  /* Indented to the title's left edge — the chevron's 20px plus the title row's gap — so
     the attached evidence reads as belonging to the action named above it. */
  .bcn-ev-row__body {
    padding-left: calc(20px + var(--spacing-300));
  }

  /* ── Drop states, ported from the Setup Wizard's actions-step.component.scss ──
     The wizard tints its drop target with --color-action (#d45087, pink), and this
     DELIBERATELY diverges from that port. In the wizard the thing being dragged IS an
     action, so the action entity colour names the payload. Here the payload is EVIDENCE and
     the action is only the destination, so the entity colour would be naming the wrong half
     of the interaction — it read as a stray pink with nothing else on the surface to
     justify it.
     --color-secondary (#00918b), not --color-primary (#005862): design-principles reserves
     saturated brand for the single primary action, which on this surface is the Attach
     button in the footer. A transient drop state should not compete with it. */
  .bcn-ev-targets__item[data-receiving] :global(.esa-card) {
    border-color: var(--color-secondary);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-secondary) 5%, transparent);
  }
  /* Already holds the evidence being dragged — a valid target for something else, but not
     for this. Dimmed and inert rather than hidden, so the list does not reflow mid-drag. */
  .bcn-ev-targets__item[data-blocked] :global(.esa-card) {
    opacity: 0.45;
  }
  .bcn-ev-targets__item[data-blocked] {
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* ── The attached evidence sub-list ── */
  .bcn-ev-attached {
    list-style: none;
    margin: var(--spacing-250, 0.625rem) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-150);
  }
  .bcn-ev-attached:empty {
    display: none;
  }
  .bcn-ev-attached__row {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--spacing-200);
    padding: var(--spacing-050) var(--spacing-200);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border-light);
    border-radius: var(--radius-200);
    background: var(--color-background);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
  }
  /* UNSAVED — the Setup Wizard's pink (--color-action #d45087) as a border plus a light
     color-mix fill, the same pairing actions-step.component.scss uses for its own live
     states. The row is the right place for it: a card can hold saved and unsaved evidence
     at once, so the treatment has to be per-attachment, not per-card.
     Named through --bcn-unsaved at the point of use because here the colour means "not
     saved", not "this is an Action" — the two meanings share a hue, and nothing else in the
     drawer is pink, which is what lets it read. */
  .bcn-ev-attached__row[data-unsaved] {
    --bcn-unsaved: var(--color-action);
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--bcn-unsaved) 45%, transparent);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--bcn-unsaved) 6%, transparent);
  }
  .bcn-ev-attached__name {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  /* The suggestion marker now lives HERE, on the individual attachment the utility made,
     not on the action card. The action was not suggested — this particular pairing was. */
  .bcn-ev-attached__mark :global(.esa-badge) {
    --badge-bg: var(--color-info-subtle);
    --badge-text-color: var(--color-text-primary);
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-info) 35%, transparent);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
  }
  /* The detach button is what SETS this row's height — at size="sm" it is 28px, more than
     the text and padding together, so trimming the padding alone barely moved the row. The
     button reads the shared form-size token, so re-pointing --form-height-sm here brings it
     down with the rest instead of leaving one control at the old scale.
     24px is still a comfortable pointer target on a dense desktop data surface; it is below
     the 44px touch guideline, which this list is not designed for. */
  .bcn-ev-attached__remove {
    flex: none;
    color: var(--color-text-tertiary);
    --form-height-sm: 24px;
  }

  /* The invitation, shown only while the card holds nothing — and drawn as a real drop
     zone rather than a line of prose. Ported from the wizard's .actions-panel__create-zone:
     dashed edge at rest over a 3% tint, centred. It reads as somewhere you can put
     something, which is exactly what it is. The wizard's tint is the action entity colour;
     this uses --color-secondary for the reason given at the drop states above. */
  /* Always dashed while the list is empty — the zone reads as somewhere you can put
     something whether or not a drag is in flight. (A drag-only variant was tried; it made
     the empty card look inert until you were already committed to a drag.) */
  .bcn-ev-row__hint {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    margin: var(--spacing-200) 0 0;
    padding: var(--spacing-200);
    border: 1px dashed var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-200);
    /* No fill. The dashed edge alone says "put something here"; a tint on top of it was
       doing the same job twice, and on the column's sunken gray it read as a stray wash
       rather than as an affordance. */
    background: none;
    color: var(--color-text-tertiary);
    /* Italic — it is an instruction about what to do with the empty space, not a label for
       something that is there. */
    font-style: italic;
    transition:
      background 120ms ease,
      border-color 120ms ease;
  }
  .bcn-ev-row__hint[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }
  /* Receiving: the edge STAYS DASHED and only takes the colour. The wizard swaps to solid
     here, and this deliberately does not — dashed is what marks the thing as a drop zone,
     so dropping it at the moment you are actually dragging over the zone removes the cue
     exactly when it is being used. Colour alone carries the state change. */
  .bcn-ev-targets__item[data-receiving] .bcn-ev-row__hint {
